| Column Name | Data Type | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| id | SERIAL | Unique identifier |
| post_id | INTEGER | Foreign key referencing the posts table |
| user_id | INTEGER | Foreign key referencing the users table |
| content | TEXT | Comment content |
| created_at | TIMESTAMP | Timestamp of comment creation |

## Relationships

* Users has many posts through the `posts` table
* Posts belongs to one user
* Posts has many comments through the `comments` table
* Comments belong to one post
* Comments belong to one user

## Indexes

* idx_users_email: index on `users` table `email` column
* idx_posts_user_id: index on `posts` table `user_id` column
* idx_comments_post_id: index on `comments` table `post_id` column

## Normalization Summary

* First normal form (1NF): each table has a unique column that can be used to identify each row, and each cell in the table contains a single value
* Second normal form (2NF): each non-key attribute of a table depends on the entire primary key
* Third normal form (3NF): if a table is in 2NF, and a non-key attribute depends on another non-key attribute, it should be moved to a separate table
* Boyce-Codd normal form (BCNF): if a table is in 3NF, and a non-key attribute depends on a single attribute within a multicolumn primary key, it should be moved to a separate table